.TH VIDEODL 1 "September 2026" "videodl" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
videodl \- download HTTP media and HLS playlists with a persistent queue
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B videodl setup
.RI [ --destination " DIR"] " [--config FILE]"
.br
.B videodl add
.RI [ options ] " [NAME] URL"
.br
.B videodl worker
.RI [ --watch ] " [options]"
.br
.B videodl
.BI --output " FILE"
.RI [ options ] " URL"
.SH DESCRIPTION
Downloads direct HTTP(S) media or public unencrypted HLS playlists.
DASH and advanced media assembly require FFmpeg.
Options must precede positional arguments. Existing output files are never replaced.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B setup
Create configuration, local guide, completions, manual and data directories.
Without options, existing settings are preserved. --destination DIR updates only
the destination, including in existing configurations. No daemon is started.
Already queued jobs keep their saved output path.
.TP
.B doctor
Read-only configuration, paths and FFmpeg diagnostics; no network access.
.TP
.B add [NAME] URL
Queue a download. Returns a job identifier.
A positional NAME, -n NAME and --name NAME are equivalent; --output/-o is also
a name alias. Do not combine these forms. Without a name, derive it from the URL.
Names must be single filenames without a directory. Quote names with spaces.
.TP
.B worker [--watch]
Drain queued jobs, or continue watching for new ones.
A failed job does not terminate continuous supervision.
Only one supervisor per queue is allowed. In watch mode, new jobs fill vacant
slots while other transfers run. Shared cancellation polling runs once per second.
.TP
.B list / status JOB_ID / watch [--once] [--json]
Inspect the queue, a job, or an automatically refreshed report.
.TP
.B retry JOB_ID / cancel JOB_ID
Requeue or cancel a job. Recoverable HTTP partial data is retained.
.TP
.B daemon start|stop|restart|status|logs
Manage the detached background worker.
.TP
.B config init|path|show|get|set|edit
Initialize, locate, inspect or edit settings. Example: config set concurrency 2.
.TP
.B completion bash|zsh|fish
Print a shell completion script. See the setup guide for installation.
.TP
.B help [COMMAND] / man
Display help or the embedded plain-text manual. General help shows the effective
configuration when available.
.SH SHORT OPTIONS
Each command accepts only its relevant options; use COMMAND -h for the exact list.
Options precede the first positional argument. Use -- before a name starting with a dash.
Values use an intervening space or equals sign: -n NAME or -n=NAME.
Grouped options and attached values such as -j8 are not supported.
Boolean values use =true or =false, for example -R=false.
.TP
.B -h, -c, -s, -l, -d
--help, --config, --state, --log, --destination.
.TP
.B -n, -o, -j, -t, -f, -F, -W
--name, --output, --concurrency, --timeout, --ffmpeg, --ffmpeg-path, --webhook.
.TP
.B -J, -w, -1, -i, -e
--json, --watch, --once, --interval, --editor.
.TP
.B -r, -R, -H, -T
--retries, --resume, --max-height, --idle-timeout.
.PP
Destination applies to add/setup, concurrency to worker, timeout and ffmpeg-path
to worker/direct downloads. Transfer options on add are saved with the job.
.SH COMPLETION
Completion offers only valid options for the selected command and cursor position.
After --name, a filename is expected and no flags are suggested.
Paths, known option values and job identifiers are completed without queue changes.
Reload completion after an upgrade in an open shell; Bash:
.PP
.nf
source <(videodl completion bash)
.fi
.SH TRANSFER OPTIONS
.TP
.B --retries N
Extra attempts on transient HTTP/network failures, 0 to 10; default 3.
.TP
.B --resume=false
Disable verified HTTP resume. Default enabled; requires a strong ETag.
.TP
.B --max-height N
HLS resolution ceiling, e.g. 1080. Zero selects the highest bandwidth.
Not supported with DASH or forced --ffmpeg.
.TP
.B --idle-timeout DURATION
Maximum idle read duration, default 60s. FFmpeg uses its network read timeout.
.TP
.B --timeout DURATION
Connection, TLS and HTTP response-header timeout, default 30s.
.TP
.B --concurrency N
Simultaneous queued downloads, 1 through 8; default 1.
.TP
.B --ffmpeg / --ffmpeg-path PATH
Force FFmpeg or select its executable. No byte resume with FFmpeg.
.SH DISK SPACE
min_free_space defaults to 2GiB. Set with config set min_free_space 2GiB,
or VIDEODL_MIN_FREE_SPACE. Integers in bytes, KiB, MiB, GiB and TiB are accepted.
Zero removes the floor but retains capacity checks.
Known HTTP sizes are reserved across active transfers of the same supervisor
and filesystem. Unknown sizes use renewable 64MiB reservations.
Native writes check periodically; FFmpeg is checked every 250ms.
This is a best-effort guard, not a quota or a reservation across processes.
Insufficient space fails the job; free space then retry JOB_ID.
Restart a running daemon after configuration changes.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/videodl/config.json
Configuration, overridden by XDG_CONFIG_HOME, VIDEODL_CONFIG or --config.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/videodl/
Queue and logs, overridden by XDG_STATE_HOME.
.TP
.I ~/Downloads/videodl/
Default destination. Configure with config set destination DIR.
.TP
.I DESTINATION/.videodl/
Private HTTP partial files, validators and persistent advisory lock files.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Settings accept VIDEODL_ uppercase names, e.g. VIDEODL_RETRIES,
VIDEODL_RESUME, VIDEODL_MAX_HEIGHT and VIDEODL_IDLE_TIMEOUT.
Precedence: defaults, configuration file, environment, command flags.
.SH UPDATES
From an up-to-date source checkout, run bin/install.sh [PREFIX].
The default prefix is ~/.local. Compilation and staging happen before service interruption.
The installer stops daemons using that installed executable and relaunches only
previously active services, preserving their profiles and private environment.
Configuration is not reinitialized and the queue is not replaced.
HTTP partials may resume; HLS and FFmpeg restart the interrupted job.
Foreground workers block replacement and must be stopped first.
Handled failures trigger restoration of previous package files and services.
Queue progress is never rolled back. Power loss and SIGKILL are not covered.
No source fetching, data-schema migration or stopped-service startup is performed.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 on invalid configuration, command error or failed one-shot job.
.SH LIMITATIONS
No website extraction or login handling. Native HLS processes a playlist snapshot.
Byte resume is for direct HTTP only; HLS and FFmpeg restart from the beginning.
HTTP partial reuse checks ETag and Content-Range; changed objects restart safely.
